="pmid">8044957</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B32"><label>32</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>CC</given-names></name><name><surname>Lugo-Olivieri</surname><given-names>CH</given-names></name><name><surname>McVeigh</surname><given-names>ER</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerhouni</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Three-dimensional systolic strain patterns in the normal human left ventricle: characterization with tagged MR imaging</article-title><source><italic>Radiology</italic></source><year>2000</year><volume>214</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>453</fpage><lpage>466</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10671594</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Short-Axis-Tagged MRI acquisition between end-diastole and end-systole.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Manual detection of epicardial (external circle) and endocardial (internal circle) boundaries of the Left Ventricle (LV) on a Short-Axis-Tagged MRI acquisition.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Radially tagged cardiac MRI taken from [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B26">26</xref>].</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>(a) Original image, (b) Norm of the corresponding gradient. As one can notice, the grid of tags does not allow us to obtain a good gradient attractor for myocardial contours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>(a) Original image <italic>u</italic><sub>0</sub>,  (b) Computation of <italic>u</italic><sub>map</sub><sup>endo</sup> with (<italic>w</italic><sub><italic>&#x003c3;</italic></sub>/<italic>w</italic><sub><italic>m</italic></sub>) = 2 and <italic>N</italic> = 11,  (c) &#x02207;<italic>u</italic><sub>map</sub><sup>endo</sup>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>(a) Original image, (b) Computation of <italic>u</italic><sub>map</sub><sup>epi</sup> with <italic>N</italic> = 5, (c) &#x02207;<italic>u</italic><sub>map</sub><sup>epi</sup>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>(a) Computation of <italic>u</italic><sub>map</sub><sup>endo</sup> with (<italic>w</italic><sub><italic>&#x003c3;</italic></sub>/<italic>w</italic><sub><italic>m</italic></sub>) = 2 and <italic>N</italic> = 11 for automatic localization of the barycenter of LV cavity, (b) initialization and detection of endocardial boundary, and  (c) initialization and detection of epicardial boundary.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Detection and tracking of endocardial and epicardial boundaries on SA sequences all along diastole for 5 different patients.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.008"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Detection mask obtained after segmentation of myocardial boundaries on a particular image: (a) expert's mask (manual segmentation), (b) automatically segmented mask (presented method), and (c) superposition of both (dark surface stands for mismatched pixels of both segmentation).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.009"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Radial contraction of the heart represented in accordance with the AHA's recommendations.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-983794.010"/></fig><table-wrap id="tab1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Average number of each type of pixels (expressed in percentage of the average total number of pixels of each pixel class within manual generated myocardium surface) for each of the 6 considered time steps of the systole.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>Systole Step</italic></th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Step  1 </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Step  2 </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Step  3 </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Step  4 </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Step  5 </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Step  6 </th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> TP </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 87.2% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 85.1% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 81.4% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 79.7% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 82.1% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 81.0%</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FP </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 3.1% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 2.9% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 2.2% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 2.1% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 1.9% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 2.1%</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TN </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 92.1% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 93.1% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 89.3%</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 90.1%</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 94.3% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 91.8% </td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FN </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 1.2%</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 1.6% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.9% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.3% </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 1.7%</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> 2.1%</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="tab2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Comparison between our quantification and two studies of the medical literature concerning the estimation of the radial displacements (expressed in millimeters) for healthy volunteers.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Base </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Median </th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Apex </th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B31">31</xref>]  (12 patients) </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.9 &#x000b1; 0.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 &#x000b1; 0.3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.65 &#x000b1; 0.2</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B32">32</xref>] (31 patients) </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.0 &#x000b1; 1.3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.3 &#x000b1; 1.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.2 &#x000b1; 1.6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"> Our estimation (10 patients)						</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.7 &#x000b1; 0.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.9 &#x000b1; 0.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.3 &#x000b1; 0.9</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></floats-group></article>